WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Speaker 1: Welcome, everybody. You may or may&nbsp;
not have heard this because you've probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:11.440
grown tired of yet another challenge to the&nbsp;
Affordable Care Act, but Obamacare, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:18.240
the same thing as the ACA, remember, Obamacare&nbsp;
has survived another challenge and this one in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.520
the Supreme Court via a seven to two decision,&nbsp;
which we're going to look at in a moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:31.280
You know, at this point, it's time to stop even&nbsp;
thinking about Obamacare as this additional thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:38.160
on our health care system. You know, it's been 11&nbsp;
years. The fundamentals of Obamacare are just now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:45.120
part of the not that great health care system that&nbsp;
we have in the United States. And increasingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.200
I mean, at this point, dozens and dozens of&nbsp;
House votes when Republicans controlled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:56.080
House to try to undo it, various different legal&nbsp;
and constitutional challenges, Donald Trump and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:02.000
four years of trying to gut it, despite the fact&nbsp;
that he had nothing sensible or reasonable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.920
replace it with. We just now have our health care&nbsp;
system and it includes some Obamacare provisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:13.120
and we now need to move forward from where we are.&nbsp;
But there are many who don't want to accept that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.080
and there are many who want to continue pretending&nbsp;
that there is still this appendage that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.880
Obamacare, which could just be clipped off very,&nbsp;
very easily. It's just part of our system at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:29.440
point in time. Let's look at some of the reporting&nbsp;
on this from CNBC. The Supreme Court ruled on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:35.520
Thursday against Texas and other Republican&nbsp;
led states seeking to strike down Obamacare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:41.440
in the law's latest test before the nation's&nbsp;
highest court. The top court voted seven to two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:46.080
to reverse an appeals court ruling that had struck&nbsp;
down the law's individual mandate provision.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.640
That is the provision that Donald Trump&nbsp;
very aggressively tried to attack, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.600
if you get rid of that, you are really doing&nbsp;
significant damage to the way Obamacare works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.120
Justice Stephen Breyer, who authored the opinion&nbsp;
of the court, said that Texas and the other states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.840
that challenged the law failed to show that they&nbsp;
were harmed by it. In legal terms, the states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.880
failed to demonstrate that they had standing.&nbsp;
Neither the individual nor the state plaintiffs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:19.360
have shown that the injury they will suffer or&nbsp;
have suffered is fairly traceable to the allegedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:25.600
unlawful conduct of which they complain. The&nbsp;
decision is a major victory for the legislation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.520
which former President Barack Obama signed in&nbsp;
2010. It's a crucial element of the nation's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:37.200
health care system. Joe Biden has vowed to expand&nbsp;
its reach. The decision marks the third time that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.960
Obamacare has survived the challenge before the&nbsp;
Supreme Court. Defenders of the law worried that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:49.120
the panel, which is now a six three Republican&nbsp;
appointed majority, would scrap the legislation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:54.320
which has long been criticized by conservatives.&nbsp;
Chuck Schumer said each time in each arena,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.800
the Affordable Care Act has prevailed.&nbsp;
Going on to say, let me say definitively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:04.000
the Affordable Care Act has won. The Supreme Court&nbsp;
has ruled the ACA is here to stay. And now we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.280
going to try to make it bigger and better. What&nbsp;
a day. What is most frustrating to me about this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:17.040
is that Obamacare did good things, but&nbsp;
it's really no incredible victory. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.040
God's gift since sliced bread or whatever&nbsp;
other metaphors and analogies you want me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:30.320
to embarrassingly mix together. We really you&nbsp;
know, I've said before, Medicare for all is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:36.000
the only way. And I've spoken recently to other&nbsp;
people on the left who have a similar perspective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.640
If you look at what Germany has done with a&nbsp;
multi payer system, if you set up a system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:49.360
where the health insurance companies exist, but&nbsp;
they must be non-profits and have to compete with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:59.600
a government plan, if you want to do a combination&nbsp;
system like France has or in Amsterdam, I'm sorry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:08.080
in in in the Netherlands, in Holland, taxes are&nbsp;
higher than in the United States and people pay a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:13.840
small fee for a comprehensive health care system.&nbsp;
At least my friend who works in Amsterdam does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.480
But overall, it's way cheaper than in&nbsp;
the United States. And you have access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:24.240
and quality is quite good. There are other ways&nbsp;
that we can get everybody coverage that is good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.080
So this is not about it has to be Medicare&nbsp;
for all, which I would be very much open to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:37.920
This is about the fact that 11 years later, we're&nbsp;
still arguing about something that is, number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:44.800
not a miraculously great thing, although it does&nbsp;
good things. And number two is not radical. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:51.120
fact that in other countries there are so&nbsp;
many phases beyond where we are and we're at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.160
half the country or at least half the elected&nbsp;
officials want to get rid of Obamacare. The other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.440
half want to keep it. Joe Biden. Wants a public&nbsp;
option, but there's real questions about whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.840
there any practical way that it's actually going&nbsp;
to be made a reality any time soon, which would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:11.440
be a great failing for Joe Biden to be absolutely&nbsp;
and completely clear. It's humiliating that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:16.080
is even the conversation that's happening in the&nbsp;
United States when you look at every other country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:21.520
that's at our level of wealth and our level of&nbsp;
development. And so, you know, when you ask me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:27.440
what is the most disgusting thing about&nbsp;
this? Is it disgusting that Republicans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:31.920
when they had the House, had like 60 something&nbsp;
votes to try to repeal Obamacare even though it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:39.760
was going to go nowhere? Yes, that's disgusting.&nbsp;
Is it disgusting that Republicans continue to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:45.040
to work within the legal system at different state&nbsp;
level courts all the way up to the Supreme Court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.440
and that Trump made it such a priority during&nbsp;
his presidency to get rid of Obamacare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.360
even though he had nothing sensible to replace it&nbsp;
with? All of that stuff is disgusting. There's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:06:00.480
question about it whatsoever. But the really crazy&nbsp;
part to me is that this is so milquetoast. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:06.640
so we listen. It's good health care dot gov. It's&nbsp;
good that it was created keeping people to age 20,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.640
up to twenty six on parent's insurance. That's&nbsp;
good. Preexisting conditions. It's good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:15.920
It's not that those things aren't good, it's crazy&nbsp;
that those things even needed to be done in 2010.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.280
But the fact that 11 years have gone by and other&nbsp;
than some really small tweaks, mostly because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:28.720
coronavirus, we are not anywhere beyond Obamacare&nbsp;
and there are still efforts being made to get rid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:34.240
of it. That's actually the disgusting part, that&nbsp;
it's something so milquetoast and unremarkable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:41.200
So Obamacare survived again in the Supreme Court.&nbsp;
Great. Now, maybe we can find something else to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:48.160
actually get done over this 11 year period during&nbsp;
which it's been basically just fending off attacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.160 --> 00:07:11.840
on Obamacare from Republicans. I don't know. I&nbsp;
don't know. We'll follow and see what happens up.

